  {
    "$id": "186",
    "$type": "TextAnnotation, Assembly-CSharp",
    "text": "The Muller pipeline acts as the backbone of most asycnrhonous circuits. It consists\nof a seties of interconnected C-gates C[i], where each gate reveives its inputs from \nthe output of its predecessor C[i-1] and the inverted of its successor C[i+1].\n\nThe pipeline basically works as a FIFO for the transitions on the request signals. When the left side issues a request, it travels trhough all the stages until it arrives at the right-hand side. If never acknowledged, the pipeline will slowly fill with every transistion on the request signal. Once filled, the C-gates will store alternating 0 and 1 vlaues and the pipeline will stop handshaking with the left hand side and block new requests from entering. The power of the Muller pipeline lies in its ability to capture all the different handshaking events.",
    "width": 215.0,
    "height": 90.0,
    "pos": {
      "$id": "187",
      "x": 95.0,
      "y": -95.0,
      "z": 0.0
    },
    "rot": {
      "$id": "188",
      "x": 0.0,
      "y": 0.0,
      "z": 0.0,
      "w": 1.0
    }
  }
